{"task_id": "HumanEval/39", "solution": "def prime_fib(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the n-th Fibonacci number that is also prime.\n    It generates Fibonacci numbers and tests their primality using Miller-Rabin.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime using the Miller-Rabin primality test.\n        This test uses a set of bases that guarantees deterministic results for numbers up to 64-bit.\n        \"\"\"\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        if num == 2 or num == 3:\n            return True\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n\n        # Deterministic Miller-Rabin bases for numbers up to 2^64 - 1 (approx 1.8 * 10^19)\n        bases = [2, 325, 9375, 28178, 450775, 9780504, 1795265022]\n\n        s = 0\n        d = num - 1\n        while d % 2 == 0:\n            d //= 2\n            s += 1\n\n        for a in bases:\n            if a >= num:\n                continue\n            x = pow(a, d, num)\n            if x == 1 or x == num - 1:\n                continue\n\n            composite = True\n            for _ in range(s - 1):\n                x = pow(x, 2, num)\n                if x == num - 1:\n                    composite = False\n                    break\n            if composite:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    count = 0\n    # Initialize Fibonacci sequence to start from F(2) and F(3)\n    # F(0)=0, F(1)=1, F(2)=1, F(3)=2, F(4)=3, F(5)=5, F(6)=8, F(7)=13, ...\n    prev_fib = 1 # Represents F(2)\n    curr_fib = 2 # Represents F(3), which is the first prime Fibonacci number\n\n    while True:\n        if is_prime(curr_fib):\n            count += 1\n            if count == n:\n                return curr_fib\n\n        # Generate the next Fibonacci number\n        next_fib = prev_fib + curr_fib\n        prev_fib = curr_fib\n        curr_fib = next_fib"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/42", "solution": "def incr_list(l: list):\n    \"\"\"Return list with elements incremented by 1.\n    Raises TypeError if any element does not support addition with 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(l, list):\n        raise TypeError(f\\\"Expected list for 'l', got {type(l).__name__}\\\")\n    result = []\n    for i, x in enumerate(l):\n        try:\n            result.append(x + 1)\n        except TypeError as e:\n            raise TypeError(f\\\"Element at index {i} (value={x!r}) does not support addition with 1\\\") from e\n    return result"}
